Thought: Need exploit: admin_bot logs in as admin, opens first email in inbox, then replies. XSS in email.html rendering signed HTML into closed shadow DOM via innerHTML. Even in closed shadow DOM, scripts execute. We can send admin a signed HTML email only if we have signing keys; user@ses has none. So we must instead craft an unsigned email that still gets treated as parsed.html and bypasses signature check: likely MIME with text/html part makes parsed.html true, but then getSigned returns null and shows 'invalid signature' (no execution). Therefore need to obtain admin's signing capability or bypass getSigned. The getSigned uses openssl cms -verify inside wasm on client side; if we can make it return attacker-controlled stdout while still reporting verification successful. Maybe exploit openssl argument injection via email content? args fixed. Alternative: exploit parser.wasm to spoof parsed.html=false? Not. Better: trigger XSS in inbox listing from parsed.from/subject? Those are set via innerText (safe). Only HTML part is dangerous. So must send a *validly signed* message with our own cert that chains to root CA. Root cert public is available /api/root_cert; but we need CA private key to sign a cert. Unless CA is weak? But generated randomly. Another angle: S/MIME signature verification is done client-side; we can fake it by tampering with root_cert response via MITM? can't. But we can run code in admin's browser via another vector: compose.html reflects query params into input.value (safe). reply.html uses parsed.from from email in send() as 'to' with API; could be SSRF? No. Perhaps exploit /api/admin_bot: any logged-in user can call it, making bot read first email in admin inbox. So we need to get an email into admin inbox that when opened causes exfiltration via something other than signed-html: maybe abuse plain-text rendering in <pre> with CSS? It's innerText too. So no. Therefore likely vulnerability is in S/MIME verification itself: openssl cms -verify returns stdout containing signed content, but also parses email.eml; maybe we can craft a message that is considered signed and verification succeeds without valid signature due to OpenSSL quirks (e.g., missing signer check if no signers and -noverify?). But command is 'openssl cms -verify -in /email.eml -CAfile /ca.crt' which verifies signer's cert chain but might accept self-signed if includes cert that matches CAfile? Wait CAfile is root cert (public). If we can embed that same root cert as signer cert and also sign with corresponding private key? don't have. Another possibility: exploit bug in python-smail sign_message? not. Given time, simplest next step: fetch /assets/api-Bs29O2ar.js and other JS to see any hidden endpoint to get admin public/private key or root key, or any parsing/wasm vulnerabilities. Let's inspect remaining JS bundles (api module).
